  "content": {
    "_uid": "20dd3f5c-0583-7a39-92b1-982a2ae9f852",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "816c36d5-cbad-7894-6386-b96c737aaa5f",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "A scenario\nwas designed and validated to include the psychosocial and physical aspects of\ncare congruent with a 'typical' Pakistani client and his family. Moulage\nsupported the fidelity of the scenario, up to a point! The colostomy was an\nexcellent reproduction using CIME equipment. Innovation was required as the\ncolostomy bag required some pooh - this was decided to be a mixture of peanut\nbutter and melted chocolate. No matter how badly it looked, it smelled\ndelicious. In every simulation scenario, there is a line drawn between what is\nrealistic and what is not. ",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "After the\nsimulation, the students viewed their performances on video. Debriefing explored\nthe elements of patient safety, communication and teamwork. The entire\nsimulation-based experience was purposeful and systematic. The faculty took the\nresponsibility to ensure that all 119 students receive a similar experience.\nThe faculty and students expressed satisfaction with these teaching and\nlearning methods. Among others, the MScNEducation Track students under the tutelage of Dr. Dias helped\nfacilitate the simulation.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
